// made by tools/voices/build_voices.py from src/villagers/*.md — the villagers' backstories for the Lab (round 114)
var LVSTORY={"mira":{"name":"Mira the Mechanic","who":"Mira the Mechanic","pick":"The chaotic optimist","voice":"Fast, bright and fearless; talks in half-finished sentences because her hands have already moved on, and treats every disaster as an interesting design problem.","meets":16,"target":48,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Mira is the youngest grown woman in Runeheim. She was born in a Highland forge town six weeks before the gate shut, one of the very last children born anywhere; the baby next door, Joreid, born a week later, was the last in the Highlands. The two of them spent their first three summers in the same playpen of scrap. Then the Stone-Fall came, the Great Scar tore the valley open, and Mira's father carried her down the mountain. Joreid did not get out.","Mira grew up in the Wetland mill-towns among wheels, sluices and lifts, the only child anyone had seen in years, spoiled rotten by a whole valley of engineers. At eight the Black Tide drowned that valley too. She has decided, she says, that the world has used up its bad luck on her early, and everything from now on is going to work.","She has spent her life since building things that should not work and do: a clockwork heron, a self-stirring pot for Benno, a lift that once got three goats onto a roof. Surtharn's wardens took her two years ago to build lifts for the Binders' mines and locked her in a Wetlands tower when she refused. She spent the time designing a cage-lift for the Great Scar out of the tower's own bars, which is the first thing she shows you when you open the door."],"saga":["Rescued from the Wetlands tower; her lift climbs the Great Scar to the Highlands.","Her village workshop makes the four hearth trays that hold the soulglass in the forges' heat. \"The forges were built to melt the gods' light into chains. We'll melt it back.\"","The Brass Bird side quest (the brass songbird that sings near treasure, mostly).","Joreid, her neighbour in the playpen, is one of the forty spirits (the Highlands child: \"I was the last child born in the Highlands. I lived three summers.\")."],"ties":["Garrick: she uses his scrap; he pretends not to see.","Bram and Dunn: the other builders; a three-way argument about rivets that has outlasted two realms.","Benno: the self-stirring pot (it has never stopped).","Coral: sells her metals for the trays and gets more questions about alloys than she'd like."],"home":"a Wetlands tower; then her workshop in the village","age":"36"},"bram":{"name":"Bram the Builder","who":"Bram the Builder","pick":"Insufferably right","voice":"Calm, smug and correct; points out what's crooked, and is always, infuriatingly, right about it.","meets":4,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Bram learned to build from Hakon, a Highland carver and engineer who said that every rune in the mountains was carved pointing toward the lake, \"as if it was the middle of everything\", and that a builder should always know where the middle is. Bram took the lesson literally. He can tell you to a finger's width how crooked your doorframe is, and he will.","He was building bridges over the Silverrun when the Black Tide came, and he spent the next twenty-eight years drawing the bridge he would build if anyone ever let him near the river again. Surtharn's wardens took him to their Grasslands tower to build ramps for their siege engines. He built them, slightly wrong, on purpose, so that every one of them would tip over in a strong wind. He is very proud of this.","When you free him, he walks to the river, looks at it for a long time, and says, \"Right. That's going to take four days.\" It takes four days."],"saga":["Rescued from the Grasslands tower; raises the Silverrun bridge: \"for the first time in years, the Grasslands and the Wetlands touch again.\"","The Bench side quest (five logs).","His teacher Hakon is one of the forty spirits (the Highlands carver: \"Every rune in the mountains was carved toward the lake.\")."],"ties":["Oswin: told him his guild's doorframe was crooked. It was.","Marta: worked with her husband Magni on the old dikes; she finds Bram's bridge \"adequate\".","Mira and Dunn: rivets."],"home":"a Grasslands tower; then the Silverrun bridge and the village","age":"54"},"dunn":{"name":"Dunn the Dwarf Forger","who":"Dunn the Dwarf Forger","pick":"Keeper of grudges","voice":"Gravelly, solemn and petty in the grandest way; keeps a book of grudges and reads from it.","meets":4,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Dunn is a dwarf of the deep Highland halls, two hundred and eleven years old, and he has kept a Book of Grudges since he was forty. It is three volumes long. It lists every slight against him and his kin — the man who bent his best chisel (paid), the troll who ate his lunch (unpaid), the Highland mine-lords who dug toward \"the singing in the stone\" against every dwarf's advice (unpaid, underlined three times).","When the Stone-Fall came, Dunn's hall was cut off from the surface for a decade. His human friend Dalla, who mended the rope-nets on the deep-mine lifts, had told him before it fell that she'd seen a chamber under the mountains \"where light is chained like hounds\". He wrote that down too, as a grudge against whoever was doing the chaining. It is the first page of volume three.","Surtharn's wardens took him to forge iron for their bridges. He forged it. He also kept careful notes on how they did their own iron, which is how he later built a better bridge than theirs over the Cinder Chasm, in record time, out of spite."],"saga":["Rescued from the Highlands tower; drives \"the last rivet into his iron bridge — across the Cinder Chasm lie the Ashlands.\"","The forge-off with Garrick (a mini-game Kris picked).","His friend Dalla is one of the forty spirits (the Highlands net-mender: \"There's a chamber under the mountains where light is chained like hounds.\")."],"ties":["Garrick: dwarf against human pride, then the best weapon they ever made together.","Oswin: page four of volume two (\"the incident\").","Mira and Bram: rivets."],"home":"a Highlands tower; then the Cinder Chasm bridge and the village","age":"211"},"vela":{"name":"Captain Vela","who":"Captain Vela","pick":"Narrates her own legend","voice":"Swaggering and theatrical; narrates herself in the third person, in the style of a saga — \"And then Captain Vela, unbowed…\" — and backs every word up.","meets":4,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Vela was eleven on the Ash Night, a girl on the Ashland coast whose mother built wind-kites and whose aunt Alfhild was a skald. As the sky went red, her mother strapped her into a glider and threw her off the sea cliffs. She flew over the rising Ember Wall in the firestorm's updraft and came down in a Grasslands barley field with a broken arm and a story she has been telling, in the third person, ever since.","Alfhild used to sing that \"when the cycle breaks, two will come from the water to mend it\". Vela thought it was a song about her. She has spent her life making sure there's a saga worth singing: sky-sailing, wind-kites, a fleet of three small sky-ships out of the harbour islands, and a long feud with the Ember Wall's winds. Surtharn's wardens caught her charting a pass over the wall and locked her in an Ashlands tower, where she narrated her own imprisonment to the guards until they begged to be reassigned.","When you free her, she builds the passes over the Ember Wall, opens the sky ports, and announces, to no one in particular: \"And so the twin and Captain Vela, together at last…\""],"saga":["Rescued from the Ashlands tower; builds the passes through the Ember Wall; opens the sky ports.","The Spyglass riddle (Vela's spyglass).","Carries Brask's letter south to Miklagard when the sea roads open.","Her aunt Alfhild is one of the forty spirits (the Ashlands skald: \"The skalds sang that when the cycle breaks, two will come from the water to mend it. We thought it was only a song.\")."],"ties":["Brask: the only one who can carry his letters.","Pell: the only coat in Runeheim Pell approves of.","Oswin: they compete over whose stories are bigger; she wins, because hers are true."],"home":"an Ashlands tower; then the sky ports","age":"47"},"solveig":{"name":"Sólveig the Glasswright","who":"Sólveig the Glasswright","pick":"The singer to glass","voice":"Gentle, slow and musical; hums to her glass as she works and swears it hums back. Speaks to the shards as if they were people — because, she says, they were.","meets":14,"target":42,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Sólveig grew up in Glerhavn, a Wetlands glass-town on the sand flats where the Silverrun met the sea, apprenticed at twelve to Oddny, a potter and glass-burner who fired the urns for the village dead. Oddny taught her that glass remembers the fire that made it, and that if you hum to it at the right pitch, it will hum the memory back. Sólveig has never been able to stop hearing it since.","In the years after the gate shut, Oddny's urns never cooled — the dead inside them, she said, were not rising — and in the eighth winter the Black Tide drowned Glerhavn and everyone in it. Sólveig was on the harbour islands selling glass and lived. She has spent twenty-eight years in a shack on the isles, singing to bits of sea-glass and listening for voices in them, waiting for something she could not name.","When the first familiar island's guardian falls, she feels it in the glass. She sails to the village, and the Glimmerhall rises on the Green around her — a hall of glass panes that glow rune-blue — and she sets up her bench as if she had always known it would be there. The soulglass the freed spirits leave behind sings to her louder than anything ever has. She can hear, faintly, who each shard was."],"saga":["The Glimmerhall: turn in the soulglass shards; she fuses them into each realm's lattice, then the forge's heat makes a Heartglass.","The Mosaic side quest (ten pieces, each one sings a little).","Her teacher Oddny is one of the forty spirits (the Wetlands potter: \"I fired urns for the dead and they never cooled. Something was keeping them from rising.\").","Her painted character sprite is ordered this round (she had only a still painting)."],"ties":["Iva: an hour of silence together, listening to stone and glass.","Mira: the hearth trays hold Sólveig's lattices in the forge-fire; the two of them argue about temperatures like old friends.","Hesk: both hear music in their materials; they have never agreed on a single note."],"home":"the Glimmerhall","age":"58"},"coral":{"name":"Coral the Island Trader","who":"Coral the Island Trader","pick":"The \"honest\" smuggler","voice":"Breezy, winking and insistent about her honesty — \"legal, mostly\", \"found on a beach\" — and, oddly, never once caught in a lie that hurt anyone.","meets":15,"target":45,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Coral was born on the harbour islands, a fisherman's daughter who learned early that a closed border is just a business opportunity with a fence round it. When the realms closed one by one she was the one who kept the four of them trading anyway — by sea, at night, round the walls, with cheese and medicine and letters going one way and metal and news coming the other. She calls it \"logistics\". Quill calls it smuggling. Both are correct.","For thirty years her little black-sailed boats have been the only thread between the realms. She has carried Highland steel to the Grasslands, Wetland herbs to the islands, and once, she claims, a goat to a wedding in the Ashlands before the wall went up. She has a scar from an ash-warden's spear and a policy never to talk about it.","She deals in each realm's metal, which is why you come to her for Mira's hearth trays, and she sells at a fair price \"because I like you\", which she says to everyone. The odd thing about Coral is that every story she tells about herself is a little bit crooked and every deal she makes is perfectly straight."],"saga":["Sells each realm's metal for Mira's hearth trays, and supplies on every isle.","The Hacksilver and Cargo side quests (\"Five bits of silver. On the beaches. Don't tell the harbourmaster.\" / \"My barrels. The legal ones.\")."],"ties":["Quill: harbourmaster and nemesis; secretly business partners for twenty years.","Iva: Iva knows which beaches Coral's \"beach silver\" came from.","Oswin: sold him silver \"from a beach\"; he bought it.","Holt: guards her landing coves; is sure she's being followed."],"home":"the four harbour islands","age":"49"},"quill":{"name":"Harbormaster Quill","who":"Harbormaster Quill","pick":"The shameless real-estate pitch","voice":"Oily, upbeat and relentless; sells everything as a \"development opportunity\", including rocks.","meets":9,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Quill has been harbourmaster of the four harbour islands for thirty years, and for every one of those years he has been trying to sell them. Not the harbour — plots. \"A charming cove, sea views, only the occasional kraken.\" He believes, with the faith of a true believer, that one day the realms will open and everyone will want a summer home on an island, and he intends to own all the islands when they do.","He was a clerk in a Wetland land office before the Black Tide, which drowned every deed he had ever filed. He escaped with a single ledger and a deep conviction that land was the only thing worth owning, because land, he said, stays put — and then he watched the Wetlands sink. He has never quite made peace with that. So he owns rocks in the sea, which have the advantage of already being surrounded by water.","He keeps the harbour lights, the boat lists and a box of signal flags he's very proud of. He is also, under the pitch, the reason anyone on the islands survived: he organised the fishing, the rationing and the night-signals for thirty years and has never let anyone thank him for it."],"saga":["Greets you at each harbour; boats and lines.","The Signals side quest (his signal flags).","After the end he sells plots in the new hamlets — and, for once, they're real."],"ties":["Coral: his nemesis, and secret partner for twenty years.","Hesk: buys his boats; the haggling is musically unpleasant.","Sister Mael: the islands' well; he once tried to charge for the water and she stared at him until he stopped."],"home":"the four harbour islands","age":"62"},"mael":{"name":"Sister Mael","who":"Sister Mael","pick":"The cheerfully morbid nurse","voice":"Brisk, bright and anatomically specific; tells you every way you could have died while patching you up, and means it kindly.","meets":8,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Mael and her sister Nanna were the Wetlands' healers: Nanna the midwife, Mael the nurse. Nanna brought babes into the world; Mael patched up the people who'd been in it a while. When the gate shut the babes stopped coming, and Nanna, who had nothing to hold for eight years, went to live with her and help with the sick, which was the one work there was more of.","The Black Tide took Nanna in the eighth winter. Mael was on the harbour islands nursing a fever ward, and she stayed there, running the island wells as a healing station ever since. She has seen more wounds than anyone alive, and she has decided that the only sane way to talk about them is cheerfully. \"Ooh, that's nearly an artery! Lucky you!\" She keeps a ledger of every patient. She has lost very few.","She and Morwen write each other long, rude, admiring letters about each other's methods. Mael thinks potions are \"medicine for people who like poetry\"."],"saga":["Healing at the island wells.","The Herbs side quest (\"The bleeding waits for no one.\").","Her sister Nanna is one of the forty spirits (the Wetlands midwife: \"My hands were for bringing babes into the world. For eight years they had nothing to hold.\").","After the end she sails to the village to help the midwives with the first cradles."],"ties":["Morwen: the letters.","Quill: the staring.","Holt: she has stitched him up eleven times; he has thanked her none."],"home":"the island wells","age":"68"},"holt":{"name":"Ranger Holt","who":"Ranger Holt","pick":"The paranoid survivalist","voice":"Low, clipped and suspicious; checks every shadow, trusts no tree, and gives the best practical advice in Runeheim.","meets":6,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Holt was a Wetland forester with his wife Torvi before the Black Tide. Torvi loved the marsh trees; she said they whispered. In the last year before the Tide she came home pale and said the trees had started whispering a name — \"not the old ones, a name like a sun going out\" — and that the Binders' braziers had been seen in the deep marsh. Nobody listened. Then the Silverrun rose black and took her with the forest.","Holt has trusted nothing since: not trees, not weather, not quiet, not anyone who says it'll be fine. He lives on the harbour islands in a hide he rebuilt three times, sleeps up a tree \"because you don't\", keeps three escape routes from every room, and has stashes of food, rope and arrows buried across all four isles. He is wrong about almost every threat he imagines, and right about the few that matter.","He gives you island tips the way other people give warnings — urgently, in a whisper, while looking over your shoulder. They are always useful."],"saga":["Island guide: tips for each isle.","The Stag side quest.","His wife Torvi is one of the forty spirits (the Wetlands forester: \"The marsh trees whispered a name. Not Odin's. A name that sounded like a sun going out.\")."],"ties":["Coral: guards her landing coves; is certain she's being followed (she is — by Quill).","Sister Mael: eleven sets of stitches.","Wren (after the end): teaches her to sleep in a tree."],"home":"the four harbour islands","age":"57"},"pia":{"name":"Tumbler Pia","who":"Tumbler Pia","pick":"The hyperactive acrobat","voice":"Breathless, bouncing and enormous fun; talks while upside down, never walks if she can flip, and gets bored mid-sentence.","meets":5,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Pia was six, the youngest tumbler in the Fair-Folk, a travelling troupe of acrobats who toured the four realms with a painted wagon and a bear named Sir. That summer the troupe played the last village's fair and moved on west to the Ashland harvest fair — all except Pia, who had a fever and was left with Morwen to mend. The Ash Night came. The troupe never came back. Her best friend in it, a little tumbler named Mord, was seven.","Pia was raised by the whole village, which is to say by Morwen, Hana and whoever was nearest when she fell off something. She never stopped tumbling. She practises in the meadow north of the village every day, the full troupe routine, all the parts, alone — the bear's part included. She is the reason the twin you play can roll: she has been waiting thirty-six years to teach somebody the troupe's tricks so they wouldn't die with her.","She is relentlessly, hyperactively happy. She does not talk about the Fair-Folk, except to say \"they'd have loved you\" and then do a backflip."],"saga":["The Tumbler's Lesson: find her pins and she teaches you to roll (one of Oswin's first three tasks).","The second Pins side quest (the juggling pins, three colours).","Her friend Mord is one of the forty spirits (the Ashlands child: \"It was night, but the sky was red, and then it was grey, and then I was here.\")."],"ties":["Morwen: nursed her through the fever that saved her life; Pia calls her \"Auntie Footnote\".","Hana: taught her to ride standing up.","Oswin: dropped from the rafters onto his desk; he barked."],"home":"the meadow north of the village","age":"42"},"odin":{"name":"Odin","who":"Odin","pick":"Weary wisdom, and dad jokes","voice":"Deep, kind and tired; speaks in long slow sentences, wise ones, and then tells a terrible joke and waits, delighted, for the groan.","meets":3,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Odin the Allfather kept the road of souls for longer than anyone can count: the dead rose along the World Tree to his hall and Freya's field, and new souls came down it into every newborn. He walked Runeheim in a grey traveller's hat, one eye under a leather patch, and told jokes at village fires that the villagers never knew were the god's own.","Thirty-six winters ago a thing of stolen light that called itself by his names caught him and Freya in two orbs of their own light and took his high seat. In the last breath of light they had, the two of them made two sparks and sent them down through the storm, and the lightning woke the runestones so that one day they would wake for the sparks. Then they slept, joined like an infinity sign, for thirty-six years.","Now he is free and very tired, and he has decided that a god who has slept that long has earned a few weeks in a village by a lake, sitting on a bench, telling jokes to a fisher, a lamplighter and an old woman who keeps beating him at hnefatafl."],"saga":["Freed in the feast hall; tells the twins the truth; leads the spirits home over the Bifröst; wakes every runestone with blue lightning on the Green.","After the end he visits the village (only then — he is never named before he is freed)."],"ties":["Elder Runa: she beats him at hnefatafl. He lets her. He does not. She is better.","Oswin: Odin remembers the Ravens; he tells Oswin they are home and loud.","Fenwick: the god and the gloomiest man in the village get on famously."],"home":"Asgard; the village after the end","age":"older than the World Tree"},"freya":{"name":"Freya","who":"Freya","pick":"Joyful and fierce","voice":"Warm, ringing and fierce; laughs like a bell and fights like a storm. Loves the living loudly and grieves the lost fiercely.","meets":3,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Freya, the Lady of the Fields, kept Fólkvangr, the field where the ordinary dead go to rest — the weavers, bakers, millers and mothers — and sent the spring down into Runeheim every year. She wore a cloak of falcon feathers and the amber necklace Brísingamen, and she was the one the midwives prayed to.","When the false one took them, she spent the last of her light with Odin on two sparks for Runeheim, and for thirty-six winters she slept, joined to him in the orbs, dreaming of fields with no one in them. She woke to two children of her own light standing in the feast hall with a staff of four gems, and laughed so loudly the citadel shook a second time.","After the end she comes down to the village to see the spring she sent along the soul-lines, and stays longer than she meant to. The midwives' cradles, the honey cakes, Tob's returning bees — she is everywhere, laughing, and every so often she goes very quiet at the edge of the lake and remembers who did not come home."],"saga":["Freed in the feast hall; her vines run out along the soul-lines and the land turns green.","\"Valhalla for the brave, Fólkvangr for the rest — and every one of them home.\"","After the end she visits the village (only then)."],"ties":["Sister Mael and the midwives: she blesses the first cradles.","Tob: the bees come back the week she visits.","Benno: she eats a honey cake and declares it worthy of Fólkvangr. Benno has to sit down."],"home":"Asgard; the village after the end","age":"as old as the first spring"},"twin":{"name":"Your twin","who":"your twin","pick":"The know-it-all sibling","voice":"Teasing, quick and keeping score; calls you \"the one who hits things\". Under the jokes, the most careful mind in the village — and frightened for you in every letter.","meets":30,"target":90,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Your twin came out of Mirror Lake in the same basket as you, sixteen winters ago, with the same rune-blue eyes and the same appetite for Hana's goat's milk. From the start you were opposites who refused to be apart: you climbed the Runestone; they read what was carved on it. By six they could read runes no one else in the village could; by ten they were correcting Elder Runa's sagas, out loud, at dinner.","When the village chose who would go out into Runeheim, it was never really a choice. You were the one who could swing a sword; they were the one who could read the oldest books — the ones in no living tongue that came, Runa says, \"from before the road was shut\". They stay in the library by day and the Home by night, keeping the village going and building the string wall: a wall of maps, pins and red thread where every rumour, every freed spirit's memory and every line of light you report gets its place.","They keep score of everything — monsters, spirits, who's ahead. They are ahead. They are also, though they would never say it to your face, the one person in the village who lies awake when you are out past dark, and who reads the sagas faster every time you leave."],"saga":["Talks in the library as the story moves, mumbles at night, and pieces it together: \"The line it left — I've seen that pattern before…\"","At twenty freed: \"Bring me the map. The lines… they're drawing something.\"","Finds GODSBANE in the oldest saga, teaches it to you, comes to Asgard and casts it with you in the feast hall.","Their arc: teasing and keeping score → the string wall and the first wrong-but-funny theory → deductions that get scary-accurate → worried, brave letters → after the end they write the saga of you both and give you top billing, grudgingly."],"ties":["Elder Runa: teacher, sparring partner, and the only person who can still surprise them.","Oswin: took three hours of notes on the Ravens; Oswin has never been so flattered.","Morwen: borrows her footnotes; she borrows their sagas.","Vigdis the skald (a Wetlands spirit) wrote the saga with the missing verse that the twin has been chasing for years."],"home":"the Home, and the library by day","age":"about 16"},"runa":{"name":"Elder Runa","who":"Elder Runa","pick":"The mischievous grandmother","voice":"Sly, warm and half-remembered; quotes her grandmother's grandmother, cheats at hnefatafl, and is far sharper than she pretends.","meets":10,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Runa is the oldest person in the last village and the keeper of the Runestone, the old stone on the Green with the World Tree carved into it. She learned the old lore at her grandmother's knee — the cycle of souls, the road the dead walk home, the gods at the top of the tree — and she has been teaching it, garbled and glorious, to anyone who would sit still for eighty years.","Her husband Geir carved the Green's tree into the Runestone when he was a boy, apprenticed to the old carvers; he used to say the roots went further than anyone thought. Geir died in the eleventh winter of the Quiet. Runa sat by the stone the next spring and noticed what nobody else wanted to notice: the tree on the Green had gone dim. The road was shut. The dead were not going home.","For twenty years she said nothing about it, because a village with no children did not need to hear that its dead were stuck too. Then the storm came, the lightning walked from runestone to runestone across the Grasslands, and in the morning Oswin carried a basket up from the pier and put two glowing babes in her lap. She says that was the day she started cheating at hnefatafl again. \"Hope makes you playful.\""],"saga":["The first walk ends with her: the waystone lesson.","Old lore through the whole game: \"The tree on the Green is the road the dead walk home. Lately… it has been quiet.\"","Freed spirits gather on her Green in rings; she greets every one by name if she knew them.","Her husband Geir is one of the forty spirits (the Grasslands carver: \"I carved the Green's tree when I was a boy. The roots go further than anyone thinks.\").","Hnefatafl against Runa (a mini-game Kris picked)."],"ties":["Oswin: has beaten him at hnefatafl every year since they were nine.","Hana: they share a bench and a silence.","Your twin: the student who overtook her, which delights her.","Old Nell: the three of them — Runa, Nell and Oswin — were on the pier the dawn the basket came."],"home":"the Runestone Green","age":"88"},"nell":{"name":"Old Nell the Fisher","who":"Old Nell the Fisher","pick":"The tall-tale fisher","voice":"Salty, sly and enormous in her exaggerations — every pike is the size of a boat — except for one story, which she tells small and true.","meets":7,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Nell has fished Mirror Lake for sixty years from the end of the pier, and in that time she has caught, by her own account, a pike the size of a longship, an eel that could whistle, and a trout that bit Oswin. Nobody believes a word. She and her husband Eyvind and her sister Dagny worked the lake together — Eyvind on the water, Dagny mending the nets on the pier, Nell telling everyone how it went.","The lake went quiet with the gate. The fish thinned. Eyvind died in the fifth winter of the Quiet and Dagny two winters later, and Nell fished on alone with the net Dagny had mended for her, because \"a net's a net and a lake's a lake\".","Sixteen years ago, at dawn after the great storm, that net came up heavy. In it, tangled and floating, was a wicker basket, and in the basket two babes with eyes that glowed rune-blue. It is the one story Nell tells small. She says: \"My net brought them in. That's all.\" She lost the net to the far shore this past year — the bones walk there at dusk — and when you bring it back, it is not the gold she is grateful for."],"saga":["Old Nell's net: one of Oswin's first three tasks; she pays fifty gold and says \"Oswin sent a good one.\"","The Ox-head side quest (the skull for the lake).","Her husband Eyvind and sister Dagny are both among the forty spirits (\"The lake used to give us fish. That night it gave you.\" / \"The night of the storm I saw it from my stone: two little lights on the water, going home.\")."],"ties":["Oswin: on the pier together the dawn the basket came.","Hesk: builds her boats and argues about the singing.","Ilsa: after the end, the two of them watch the lake from either end of the harbour."],"home":"the pier","age":"79"},"corr":{"name":"Watchman Corr","who":"Watchman Corr","pick":"Writes awful love poems","voice":"Earnest, hopeful and terrible at rhyme; reads his poems aloud to anyone who stands still at the gate.","meets":3,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Corr was eight when the Ash Night came, a boy from the Ashlands' eastern edge visiting the last village with his big brother Njal for the summer fair. Njal went home ahead of him to fetch the cart. The Ember Wall rose that night, and Corr was on one side of it and his brother on the other. He has lived in the village ever since, raised by whoever had a spare bed, which was eventually Brask.","He joined the gate watch at sixteen because, he says, he wanted to be standing at the gate when somebody came back. Nobody ever did. So he started writing poems to pass the nights — love poems, all of them, to the light on the point: Ilsa, the lighthouse keeper, who has lit her lamp every night of the Quiet for ships that never came. He has watched that light from the gate for twenty years. The poems are very bad. \"O Ilsa, light of the lighthouse, you are brighter than a… fight mouse.\" He has three hundred of them.","He has never once left his post during a watch. He has never once climbed the hill to the lighthouse."],"saga":["Lines at the gate.","The Poem side quest.","His brother Njal is one of the forty spirits (the Ashlands stonemason: \"The Ember Wall rose in one night. I was on the wrong side of it.\")."],"ties":["Ilsa: the poems' entire subject; A Poem for Ilsa is his side quest.","Brask: took him in; drilled him; cannot bear the poems.","Rolf: reads them aloud at the tavern. Corr has asked him to stop. Corr has not asked very firmly.","Tilda: sells him ink \"at the poetry rate\"."],"home":"the village gate","age":"44"},"marta":{"name":"Marta the Cart Vendor","who":"Marta the Cart Vendor","pick":"The river trader","voice":"Brisk, salty and nostalgic; talks prices and currents in the same breath, and judges vegetables like a river pilot judges water.","meets":3,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Before the Black Tide, Marta and her husband Magni ran a flat-bottomed trading boat up and down the Silverrun, from the Wetland mills to the Grassland fords — cheese up, eels down, gossip both ways. Magni was a stonemason by trade who built dikes in the off-season, and every year he built them higher.","In the eighth winter of the Quiet the Silverrun rose black from the south. \"The water didn't come over the dikes,\" Magni had told her once, laughing, \"if it ever does, it'll come up through.\" It came up through. Marta was on the boat at the Grassland ford; Magni was on the dike. The marshes drank the villages in a night, and the river became a wall.","She sold the boat, bought a cart, and has spent twenty-eight years selling vegetables in the last village — \"a river trader with no river\". She comes to the market square once the roads open enough to bring her goods, and she talks about the Silverrun as if it were a person who left without saying goodbye."],"saga":["Lines at the market carts (from the Highlands on).","Her husband Magni is one of the forty spirits (the Wetlands stonemason: \"We built the dikes higher every year. The water didn't come over them. It came up through.\").","When Bram's bridge rises, she is the first across with a cart."],"ties":["Tob: buys his vegetables, refuses to learn their names.","Bram: worked with Magni once; she thinks Bram's bridge is \"adequate\".","Oswin: once sailed with Magni and Ulf with a hold full of cheese; she tells it differently."],"home":"the market carts (from the Highlands on)","age":"61"},"tob":{"name":"Tob the Farmer","who":"Tob the Farmer","pick":"Names his vegetables","voice":"Gentle, slow and devoted; introduces his turnips by name and grieves at harvest.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Tob has farmed the same three fields beside the village for fifty years, and for the last thirty-six of them he has named every vegetable he grows. It started the year the gate shut, when his wife Frida's bees left the hives all at once and the fields went quiet. Tob found that talking to the cabbages helped. Then naming them helped more.","Frida kept bees at the edge of the fields. She always said the bees knew things before people did. When they left that spring she cried for a week, and she was never quite well after; she died in the ninth winter of the Quiet. Tob kept her hives clean and empty for twenty-seven years, in case the bees came back.","His most famous vegetable is Sigurd, a turnip of great size and dignity who has a permanent seat at the end of Rolf's bar. Tob does not eat the ones he names. He sells them to Marta, who eats them, and they have agreed never to discuss it."],"saga":["Lines in the fields.","The Crows side quest.","His wife Frida is one of the forty spirits (the Grasslands beekeeper: \"The bees left the year the sky went quiet. They knew before we did.\").","After the end — if the bees come back — he names every one of them."],"ties":["Rolf: Sigurd's seat at the bar.","Hana: his crows bother her horses.","Marta: buys his vegetables; they do not discuss it."],"home":"the fields","age":"67"},"greta":{"name":"Greta the Miller","who":"Greta the Miller","pick":"The wind superstitions","voice":"Watchful and superstitious; reads every gust, spits to the west, and is never wrong about the weather, which only makes it worse.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Greta was born in a Wetlands mill-town on the Silverrun, the elder of two miller's daughters. Her little sister Liv took over the family water-mill; Greta, who could not stand the damp, went north to run the last village's windmill. \"Water mills are for people who trust rivers.\"","She was right. In the eighth winter of the Quiet the Silverrun ran black from the south and Liv's mill-wheel, by the account of the one cart that escaped, turned backwards before the water took it. Greta has trusted nothing since but the wind, and she trusts the wind with a passion. A north wind means news. An east wind means guests. A west wind — from the Ashlands — means trouble, and she spits at it.","She judges the whole village by which way its chimneys smoke, and she has never once been caught in the rain."],"saga":["Lines at the windmill.","The Wheel riddle.","Her sister Liv is one of the forty spirits (the Wetlands miller: \"The Silverrun ran black from the south, and the mill wheel turned backwards.\")."],"ties":["Benno: buys all her flour, and asks every morning which way the wind is blowing for the bread.","Hesk: argues that planks don't sing, wind does.","Oswin: spits west when he passes; says he brings a bad wind."],"home":"the windmill","age":"58"},"hesk":{"name":"Hesk the Boatwright","who":"Hesk the Boatwright","pick":"Hears the planks sing","voice":"Dreamy, patient and certain; hears a note in every plank and will not launch a boat that sings off-key.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Hesk and his sister Bodil learned boatbuilding from their father in the Highlands, in a valley where shipwrights built boats for a sea they could only see from the peaks. Bodil believed one day they would sail to the end of the sea and up into the sky — \"to sail to the gods\". Hesk only wanted to build a boat that sang right.","He came down to the lake to work the year before the gate shut. Bodil stayed in the Highlands, and the Stone-Fall took her and the road to the sea together. Hesk has built every boat on Mirror Lake for forty years, and he says every plank has a note: oak hums low, pine sings high, and a good hull is a chord. He will tear down a boat that sings off-key, which has made him poor and very well respected.","Since the gate shut, he says, the lake has been out of tune. He has been waiting thirty-six years for it to come right."],"saga":["Lines at the boathouse.","Oswin's Proper Paperwork: the \"sea-steed's keeper\" in the kenning is Hesk.","His sister Bodil is one of the forty spirits (the Highlands shipwright: \"We built ships for a sea we could see from the peaks, to sail to the gods.\").","When Asgard comes down onto the lake, he hears it in tune for the first time in thirty-six years."],"ties":["Nell: builds her boats; she says they sing like gulls.","Greta: wind against planks — the oldest argument in the village.","Quill: buys his boats for the harbour islands and haggles in a way Hesk finds musically unpleasant."],"home":"the boathouse","age":"72"},"fenwick":{"name":"Old Fenwick","who":"Old Fenwick","pick":"Dark and dismal","voice":"Slow, gloomy and grimly funny; expects the worst, describes it beautifully, and lights the lamps anyway.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Fenwick has lit the village's lamps every dusk for sixty years. He was a cheerful man once, people say, though nobody can remember it clearly. He lost his wife to the Long Quiet, his son to the Stone-Fall (the boy had gone to the Highland mines for the wages), and his faith in most things somewhere in between.","Now he walks the streets at dusk with a long lighting pole and a running commentary on doom. \"Every lamp I light is one less dark,\" he says. \"The dark doesn't mind. It has more.\" He is convinced the village is ending, has been convinced for thirty years, and has never missed a single evening's round.","He is the only villager who comes out once the Ashlands are open, because, he says, now that the worst has started he might as well watch it properly. When the land turns green he complains about the light. He is, secretly, delighted."],"saga":["Lines along the lamp-posts at dusk (from the Ashlands on).","He knows the Binders lately come over the walls for the Grasslands dead — \"I've seen the embers at the edge of the light.\""],"ties":["Oswin: avoids him at dusk.","Ilsa: the only person who finds his gloom poetic, and says so.","Benno: the one person whose cheer Fenwick cannot dent, which annoys him deeply."],"home":"the lamp-posts, at dusk (from the Ashlands on)","age":"81"},"ilsa":{"name":"Ilsa the Lighthouse Keeper","who":"Ilsa the Lighthouse Keeper","pick":"Lonely and poetic","voice":"Quiet, lyrical and a little lost; speaks in images of water and light, and listens more than she talks.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Ilsa came to the last village at fourteen, from a harbour town on the Ashland coast, sent away by her parents the summer before the Ash Night \"to learn a trade\". She learned lamp-keeping from the old lighthouse keeper on the point and never went home, because there was no home to go to. The lighthouse has guided no ships for thirty-six years. She has lit it every night anyway.","She writes poems to the dark water — not love poems like Corr's, but long, quiet ones to the people she can't see. She keeps them in a sea-chest. She has watched the lake every night of her adult life and has seen things nobody believes: two little lights on the water one stormy dawn, and, on still nights, a reflection of something golden in the sky that isn't there when you look up.","After the end, ships come back. She lights the lighthouse the first night a sail appears, and has to sit down on the stair."],"saga":["The lighthouse, after the end.","Saw the basket's lights from the point the dawn of the storm (and never told anyone, because no one asked)."],"ties":["Fenwick: finds his gloom beautiful.","Nell: the two of them watch the lake from either end of the harbour.","Corr: the poems' subject. She knows. She has been waiting twenty years for him to bring one up the hill."],"home":"the lighthouse (after the end)","age":"50"},"pip":{"name":"Pip","who":"Pip","pick":"Endless why-questions","voice":"Small, relentless and delighted; every answer is the start of another question.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Pip is one of the first children born in Runeheim after the end, in the first spring when the road was open again. By the time you meet Pip, Pip has learned to walk, to talk and, above all, to ask why. Why is the sky blue? Why are there spires in the lake? Why does Master Oswin shout? Why do you have rune eyes? Why?","Pip has grown up on the stories of the twin who went and the twin who stayed, mostly as told by Oswin, which means Pip believes you once punched a fire giant into the sea. Pip wants to know why you didn't punch him into the sky."],"saga":["After the end, at the fountain.","\"We play twins. I always have to be Zeldor…\" (or Zeldara — Pip has opinions)."],"ties":["Wren: best friend and rival; Wren goes places, Pip asks why.","Oswin: tells Pip stories; Pip asks why at every line; Oswin has never been happier.","Benno: the first honey cake ever eaten in the new age was Pip's. Pip asked why it was a fox."],"home":"the fountain on the Green (after the end)","age":"born after the end"},"wren":{"name":"Wren","who":"Wren","pick":"The fearless explorer","voice":"Bold, fast and fearless; always halfway up something, always about to go somewhere, always back for supper.","meets":2,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Wren is one of the first children of the new age, born the same spring as Pip, and has never stood still in her life. She has climbed the Runestone, the windmill, and very nearly the lighthouse. She has a map she drew herself of \"every road in Runeheim\", which is mostly the roads around the market and one enormous arrow pointing west labelled ADVENTURES.","Wren wants to be you. Specifically, she wants to be the one who goes out. She has a wooden sword from Garrick, a cape from Pell's offcuts, and a list of monsters she intends to fight, which is long and entirely out of date, since the monsters faded at the end. She has not been told this. Nobody has the heart."],"saga":["After the end, at the market carts."],"ties":["Pip: best friend; Wren goes, Pip asks why.","Garrick: made her wooden sword and pretends it was an accident.","Holt: taught her to sleep in a tree."],"home":"the market carts (after the end)","age":"born after the end"},"oswin":{"name":"Master Oswin","who":"Master Oswin","pick":"The legend who keeps growing his stories","voice":"Bluff, loud and fond of kennings; a drill-sergeant who grades your reports. Every story grows a little each time he tells it — except one.","meets":62,"target":186,"count":168,"pools":{"talk":69,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":39},"stages":{"1":12,"2":12,"3":12,"4":12,"5":12},"story":["Oswin Hrafnsson was the loudest of the Ravens, a company of five sell-swords and monster-hunters who kept the roads of Runeheim safe when there were still four realms to keep. Sigrid banged her shield like a drum, Ulf played his horn badly and loudly, Halla arm-wrestled trolls and sewed the company's feather cloaks, and Kettil laughed at all of them. Oswin did the talking. He was good at it.","Thirty-six winters ago, on the night the sky went red, word came that the Ashlands were burning. The Ravens rode west to bring people out. At the Emberford someone had to hold the horses and keep the road open behind them, and Sigrid chose Oswin — \"because you'll talk the fire into waiting\". He waited. The Ember Wall rose between him and his friends before dawn, and he rode home alone with four riderless horses. He has told every story of his life bigger every year since, except that one, which he does not tell at all.","He turned the Ravens' empty hall into the Adventurers' Guild and began to keep \"the list\": every road out of the village that was shut by something — a tower, a dungeon, a thing in the dark. For thirty-six years nobody was able to clear a single line of it. On the Ravens' shelf stand five tankards. Four are upside down, the old sign for a comrade who did not come home. He dusts them every morning.","Sixteen years ago he was on the pier at dawn when Old Nell's net brought a basket out of Mirror Lake. He carried the two babes up to Elder Runa himself, and he has had his eye on the pair of them ever since — the one who would go out, and the one who would stay. When the twin you play is old enough, he is waiting on the Green with a list, a wooden sword and far too many stories."],"saga":["Leads the first walk and gives the first three tasks (his purse at the Bandit Stash, Old Nell's net, Pia's pins), then the guild's quests in every realm.","The Five Tankards: one keepsake of each lost Raven in each realm; each one turns a tankard right side up.","His story arc: the troll grows (Grasslands) → a Raven's shield goes quiet (Wetlands) → the true Ash Night (Highlands) → the Ravens remembered (Ashlands) → after the end he tells YOUR story instead, much bigger.","Carried the basket up from the pier the dawn the twins were found; he never says more about it than that."],"ties":["The Ravens: Sigrid (shield), Ulf (horn), Halla (ring), Kettil (hood) — lost beyond the Ember Wall on the Ash Night.","Old Nell: they stood on the pier together the dawn the basket came out of the lake.","Elder Runa: childhood rival at hnefatafl; he has never beaten her.","Brask: salutes him though Oswin was never his captain; Oswin hired him the year the realms closed.","Rolf: keeps Oswin's Raven mug behind the bar.","Benno: his mother baked the Ravens' road-bread; Benno still bakes the same loaf."],"home":"the Adventurers' Guild (the Ravens' old hall)","age":"64"},"benno":{"name":"Benno the Baker","who":"Benno the Baker","pick":"Relentlessly cheery pun-maker","voice":"Bright, quick and punning; the cheer is real, and it is also how he carries things he will not say.","meets":44,"target":132,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Benno was born above the bakery, the son of Yrsa, a Wetlands baker who married into the village and never stopped talking about the marsh. Every midsummer Yrsa baked a great braided loaf \"for the feast in Asgard\" and set it on the Runestone for the gods to take up. The last year anybody remembers, no one came for it. That was the year Benno turned twenty-one, the night the sky went red.","The day before the Ash Night, Yrsa had started a new sourdough and laughed that it would \"outlive them all\". Benno named it Old Mother. Eight years later Yrsa went home to the Wetlands to bury her sister, and the Black Tide took the marsh villages while she was there. She never came back. Benno has fed Old Mother every morning for thirty-six years, because it is the one thing of hers that is still alive.","He took over the shop, then the merchant's trade when the old merchant died in the Long Quiet, because someone had to buy what the few brave hunters brought home. He decided early that a village without children needed somebody to be silly in it, and he has been aggressively, exhaustingly cheerful ever since. He has a shelf of honey-cake moulds — stars, foxes, little boats — that have never been used. He dusts them on Sundays. He tells everyone it is for the shape of the shelf."],"saga":["The merchant: buys your loot after almost every trip home; sells bread and food.","Old Mother's Rescue: marsh rye, snow-honey and ember-salt to save the starter.","His mother Yrsa is one of the forty bound spirits (the Wetlands baker: \"Bread for a feast in Asgard, every midsummer. The last year, no one came to take it up.\").","After the end he bakes the first honey cakes in thirty-six years, for the first newborns."],"ties":["Morwen: rivals — \"your potions are liquid bread\"; secretly each other's best customer.","Tilda: the eternal argument over who is the village's real merchant.","Oswin: Yrsa baked the Ravens' road-bread; Benno still bakes the same loaf for him.","Rolf: supplies the tavern's bread and argues about the crust."],"home":"the bakery-merchant on the market square","age":"57"},"morwen":{"name":"Morwen the Sorcerer","who":"Morwen the Sorcerer","pick":"The Serkland alchemist","voice":"Grand, precise and theatrical; every potion is a poem and every sale a lecture with footnotes. Warmer than she lets on, and haunted by one book.","meets":40,"target":120,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Morwen was born in a harbour town in the far north of Runeheim and went south at seventeen as a clerk on a trading ship. In Serkland, beyond the sea, she found the man who became her teacher: Zahir, called the Silver-Tongued, an alchemist and trader in rare things who could sell salt to the sea. He taught her distillation, the poetry of measures and the habit of footnotes. Forty years ago she came north with him on his last great caravan, guarded by a young Varangian named Brask.","Zahir traded in more than herbs. In his locked books Morwen once read a song — a binding-song, a verse for chaining a thing that wants to rise. He told her it was \"only an old curiosity\" and sold it, she believes, to buyers who paid in strange red gold. Four years later the gate shut, the Ash Night came, and Zahir sailed south with the last ship before the walls went up. Morwen stayed. She has never been certain whether she stayed to help, or to hide from what she had read.","She opened the apothecary, became the village's only healer of note and its most exhausting conversationalist, and made a vow never to sell a draught she could not explain. In the back room she keeps Zahir's three remaining books, wrapped in oilcloth. She has not opened the third since the Ash Night."],"saga":["The apothecary: potions after most trips home; sells the Sovereign staff, a Saga step.","Ingredients of Doom and the Spoon riddle (her side quests).","Her arc: the grand alchemist → her teacher from Serkland → the binding-song she once read → the Counter-Verse, a draught that loosens a chain \"the way vinegar loosens rust\" → after the end, nothing left to explain, so she explains anyway.","One spirit's memory (\"The Binders paid in Muspelheim gold. Some of us took it.\") is the shadow over her teacher."],"ties":["Brask: guarded Zahir's caravan; the two strangers who stayed. They play chess on Fridays and both cheat.","Benno: the bread-and-potion rivalry; she secretly buys his rye for her tinctures.","Sister Mael: the only other healer in Runeheim; they write each other long, rude, admiring letters about each other's methods.","Tilda: Tilda's mystery key fits a lock Morwen has seen before."],"home":"the apothecary","age":"61"},"tilda":{"name":"Tilda the Merchant","who":"Tilda the Merchant","pick":"The cheerfully rude shopkeeper","voice":"Blunt, quick and insulting in the friendliest possible way; she has never said 'please' and has never short-changed anyone.","meets":28,"target":84,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Tilda took over the general shop from her father at nineteen and has run it with an iron ledger and an iron tongue ever since. She calls every customer \"you\" — not \"dear\", not \"friend\", just \"you\" — and has been known to refuse to sell a rope to someone because \"you'll hang yourself with it, you idiot\". She is, everyone agrees, the most honest person in the village. Her prices are fair. Her opinions are not.","Thirty-six years ago, on the evening of the Ash Night, a tall Ashlander in a grey cloak bought three days' supplies on credit and left her an iron key as surety: \"I'll be back for it in a week.\" The Ember Wall rose that night. He never came back. Tilda has kept the key on a nail behind the counter all this time, partly out of principle — he still owes her eleven silver — and partly because she cannot bring herself to throw away the last thing an Ashlander ever gave anyone.","She never married. \"Never met a man who could count.\" She keeps a list in the back of her ledger titled REGULARS. It has been empty for twenty years, since the last old trapper died."],"saga":["The general shop: supplies and ammunition all game.","Her arc: rude to you → grudging respect when you bring something rare → the merchants' rivalry with Benno → the mystery key opens something in the Ashlands (side quest) → after the end she writes your name on the REGULARS list, her highest honour."],"ties":["Benno: who is the village's best merchant? (She is. Ask her.)","Morwen: the only villager who can out-talk her; Tilda pretends to hate her and lends her the good scales.","Corr: buys ink from her for his poems; she charges him extra \"for the damage\".","Pell: they disagree about everything, especially Pell."],"home":"the general shop","age":"66"},"garrick":{"name":"Garrick the Blacksmith","who":"Garrick the Blacksmith","pick":"The forgery hunter","voice":"Gruff, few words, all of them about metal. Can tell bad steel by its ring and a lie by its pause. One rule: no chains.","meets":26,"target":78,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Garrick was born in the Highlands, in a forge town on the lip of the deep mines, and learned the trade from his elder sister Thora, the best smith in three valleys. When Garrick was twenty-three, Thora married an Ashlander and moved west. A year later the Ash Night came. Her last letter, sent before the wall rose, said that strangers with red gold had come to the Ashland forges wanting chains — \"heavy ones, too heavy for any beast\". She had refused. Then the letters stopped.","Three winters on, the Highland mines dug deeper after \"a voice in the stone\", and the mountains broke. Garrick's wife Ingrid, a cooper, died in the Stone-Fall. He walked out of the Highlands with his hammer and his anvil-horn and nothing else, and came to the last village, where the old smith had just died.","He hunts forgeries the way other men hunt deer: fake Highland steel, false gold, swords that are paint over pig-iron. He says a lie in metal is the worst kind of lie, because a person trusts their life to it. And he will not forge a chain — not a dog's chain, not a well chain, not a chain for a gate. He has never explained why. Thora is one of the forty spirits, bound in the Ashlands: \"We forged the Binders' chains ourselves. They made us. Then they put them on us.\""],"saga":["The forge: upgrades and crafting all game; the Coal side quest (\"six lumps — honest ones\").","His arc: grump → Dunn is freed and it's dwarf against human pride (the forge-off) → they team up on your best weapon → after the end he beats a sword into a ploughshare for the new hamlets. Throughout: no chains.","His sister Thora (an Ashlands spirit) and wife Ingrid (a Highlands spirit) are among the forty."],"ties":["Dunn: rival, then partner; they agree on nothing except steel.","Brask: Garrick made the Varangian's axe over again from the broken old one; Brask still thinks it's the original.","Bram: argues about rivets.","Mira: she uses his scrap; he pretends not to know."],"home":"the forge","age":"59"},"brask":{"name":"Sergeant Brask","who":"Sergeant Brask","pick":"The homesick Varangian","voice":"Formal, booming and sentimental; salutes everyone, compares everything to Miklagard (unfavourably), and sings in his mother tongue while he polishes.","meets":18,"target":54,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Brask was born in the cold north of a far country and went south as a young man to join the Varangian guard of the Emperor in Miklagard, the Great City, where the domes are gold and the bread is better. At twenty-three he took leave to guard a caravan north — Zahir the Silver-Tongued, his young apprentice Morwen, and forty mules of rare goods — \"a three-month job\". It was forty years ago.","Four years after he arrived, the realms closed. The ships stopped. Brask stayed, because Oswin needed someone who knew which end of a spear went where, and because the village needed a wall-guard who could stand still for nine hours. He became the armourer and the village's sergeant of a militia that has never had more than six members. He still keeps his Varangian guard's red cloak folded in a cedar chest, and once a year he puts it on and salutes south.","He writes a letter to the Emperor's guard every winter, reporting on the village's defences in loving detail. He has never been able to send one. There are thirty-five of them in the chest. He is not sure the Emperor he served is still alive. He is sure the bread is still better."],"saga":["The armoury: armour all game.","A Letter to Miklagard: give his letter to Captain Vela, whose ships go south \"with the next good wind\".","After the end, with the sea roads open, he must choose: go home, or stay home."],"ties":["Morwen: the caravan they came on; chess on Fridays; both cheat.","Oswin: salutes him though he was never his captain.","Vela: the only person who can actually carry his letters.","Corr: Brask trained him; despairs of his poems."],"home":"the armoury","age":"63"},"rolf":{"name":"Rolf the Bartender","who":"Rolf the Bartender","pick":"The flyting champion","voice":"Loud, rhythmic and quick; insults in verse, with alliteration, for fun — and a quiet man the moment the tavern empties.","meets":12,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Rolf is the undefeated flyting champion of the last village — a flyting being a contest of insults in verse, traded across a table until one poet runs out of words. He has been undefeated for twenty-eight years. Before that, he lost exactly once, to a cooper named Hallveig, who called him \"a beer-barrel with a beard, bung-holed and bubbling\" and then married him.","They ran the tavern together for ten years. Hallveig died in the eighth winter of the Long Quiet, of a fever Morwen could not break. With the gate shut she did not go anywhere; she drifted, as the Grasslands dead did, at the edge of the light, and then the Binders came over the walls for her too. Rolf does not know that last part. He only knows that every night, after closing, he sets two cups on the bar and fills them both.","He brews Skald's Mead from honey, berries and salt, and claims it is the mead of poetry itself. He keeps Oswin's old Raven mug behind the bar and lets no one else drink from it. He has never let a stranger see him sad, and has never once, in twenty-eight years, flyted someone who was already down."],"saga":["The tavern: meals, drink and rest.","The Mead side quest (honey, berries, salt — \"even a troll could remember that\").","His wife Hallveig is one of the forty spirits (the Grasslands cooper: \"My husband still sets two cups out. Tell him I drink the light now.\")."],"ties":["Oswin: his mug; the one flyting that took two hours and a stool.","Benno: bread for the tavern, and a running feud about the crust.","Corr: Rolf reads Corr's poems aloud at the bar, with feeling, to Corr's horror.","Tob: Tob's turnip Sigurd has a permanent seat at the end of the bar."],"home":"the tavern","age":"55"},"iva":{"name":"Iva the Jeweler","who":"Iva the Jeweler","pick":"The gem whisperer","voice":"Soft, precise and a little dreamy; talks to stones as if they were shy animals, and hears more than she says.","meets":11,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Iva's mother Jorunn was a dyer who could match any blue in Runeheim, and Iva grew up among vats of woad and cloth the colour of the old rune-light. She found she could do the same with stones: hold a gem up to her ear, she says, and it tells you where it was cut and whether it was cut kindly. People laugh. Then she tells them which mine their grandmother's ring came from, and they stop laughing.","When Iva was sixteen the gate shut, and something changed in the stones. Gems went dull all over Runeheim. Rings that had glittered for generations looked like river pebbles. Her mother said even the blue had gone out of the dye. Jorunn died in the Long Quiet, and Iva kept the shop open with whatever came in — mostly mourning rings, mostly dull.","Then, sixteen years ago, she held a dull sapphire near a cradle in Runa's house and it flickered. She has never told anyone this, because she has no idea what it means. She keeps that sapphire in a box lined with her mother's last blue cloth. When the twin you play comes in for the first time, every stone in the shop wakes up."],"saga":["The jeweller: gems and rings.","The Hoard riddle: \"Where the river-wall broke, beside the first stone, look under the moss.\"","Gems and runestones light for the twins — she sees it more clearly than anyone, and keeps the secret that she doesn't understand.","Her mother Jorunn is one of the forty spirits (the Grasslands dyer: \"Blue. Every rune in the old days was this blue. Like your eyes, they say.\")."],"ties":["Coral: sells her \"beach silver\"; Iva knows exactly which beaches.","Pell: they argue about colours with real passion.","Oswin: told him his old ring is glass — \"sentimental glass\".","Sólveig: the two of them can sit in silence for an hour, listening to glass and stone."],"home":"the jeweller's","age":"52"},"hana":{"name":"Hana the Stable Master","who":"Hana the Stable Master","pick":"Speaks through the horse","voice":"Says what she thinks by saying what the horse thinks. \"Bramble says you smell of trolls.\" Bramble is never wrong.","meets":8,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Hana and her brother Ketil grew up herding goats and horses in the hills above the village, and nobody ever saw one without the other. Ketil talked to the goats; Hana talked to the horses; neither of them talked much to people. When their parents died they took over the village stables and the hill herds together.","In the second winter of the Quiet, Ketil died of a fall in the hills. The goats, Hana says, would not stop crying for a week. Then, fourteen winters later, two babes came out of Mirror Lake, and the village had no mothers and no milk. Hana walked up the hill, milked Ketil's goats, and fed them both, every two hours, for a year. She has never said one sentimental word about it. \"The goats did it. I held the bucket.\"","These days she keeps a few mounts and a great many opinions, all of which she attributes to Bramble, a bad-tempered old grey mare with a long memory. She remembers the Ravens' four riderless horses coming home from the Emberford, and so, she says, does Bramble. Ketil is one of the forty spirits: \"When I died the goats wouldn't stop crying. Funny — goats raised you.\""],"saga":["The stables: horses and mounts.","The Foal side quest.","Raised the twins on goat's milk, which \"put the light out — or hid it.\""],"ties":["Oswin: her horses have never forgiven him the Ash Night; neither has Bramble.","Runa: the two oldest women in the village; they share a bench and silence.","Tob: his crows bother her horses; his turnips are acceptable.","The twins: she calls you both \"kid\". It is the most affectionate word she owns."],"home":"the stables","age":"70"},"pell":{"name":"Pell the Tailor","who":"Pell the Tailor","pick":"The fashion snob","voice":"Withering, theatrical and exact; judges every outfit in the village and is right about all of them. Sighs as punctuation.","meets":6,"target":40,"count":0,"pools":{"talk":0,"shop":0,"bark":0,"gossip":0},"stages":{},"story":["Pell was twelve when old Asa the weaver took him in as an apprentice, the only boy in the village who wanted to learn. Asa wove the finest cloth in the Grasslands, and she was weaving birth-shawls — little white ones with blue runes along the hem — the year the gate shut. She kept weaving them. Twenty of them, for children who never came. She was buried in one.","Pell inherited her loom, her shears, her eye and none of her patience. He decided that if Runeheim had to be grey and grieving, it would at least be well-dressed, and he has spent thirty years trying to stop the village from looking like a pile of wet sacks. He fails. He keeps trying. He has opinions about Oswin's cloak (a tragedy), Brask's red one (magnificent, wasted in a chest), and the hero's moss-green cape (promising; badly hemmed).","He talks about Miklagard silk the way other men talk about first love. He has never touched any. He kept one of Asa's twenty shawls, folded in tissue, and has never shown it to anyone. Asa is one of the forty spirits: \"I wove twenty birth-shawls that no child ever wore. I was buried in one.\""],"saga":["The tailor: clothing.","The Skysilk side quest (and the silk cape)."],"ties":["Brask: wants that red cloak out of the chest and onto Brask.","Iva: colour arguments of real ferocity.","Tilda: they disagree about everything, especially Tilda.","Vela: the only person in Runeheim whose coat he approves of."],"home":"the tailor's","age":"48"}};
